        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target Fact:** `F:ml430-nat-modeq-symm-0a3d4d18` (Nat.ModEq.symm)\n\n**Formal Statement:** `∀ {n a b : ℕ}, a ≡ b [MOD n] → b ≡ a [MOD n]`\n\n**Structural Shape:** This is a symmetry property for the modular equivalence relation on natural numbers. It states that if `a` is congruent to `b` modulo `n`, then `b` is congruent to `a` modulo `n`. The goal has:\n- Three implicit natural number parameters (n, a, b)\n- One hypothesis: `a ≡ b [MOD n]`\n- One conclusion: `b ≡ a [MOD n]`\n\n**Dependencies:** No dependencies (empty depends_on list), making it dependency-ready.\n\n**Unlocks:** Would unlock two downstream facts: `Nat.ModEq.comm` and `Nat.ModEq.dvd_iff`.\n\n**Available Premises:** The nat prelude contains `Nat.mod_eq_symm` which provides symmetry for `Nat.modEq`, plus general symmetry theorem `Eq.symm`. No `Nat.ModEq*` theorems exist in the kernel yet.\n\n**Similar Eligible Facts:** The frontier contains related modular equivalence properties including `modeq-refl`, `modeq-trans`, `modeq-add-left`, `modeq-add-right`, and various cancellation/operation lemmas. There's also `coprime-symmetric` with similar symmetry structure. Integer versions (`Int.ModEq`) also appear.\n\n**Existing Operations:** No registered operations target this fact.",
        "id": null,
        "provider_name": null,
        "provider_details": null,
        "part_kind": "text"
      }
    ],
